<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-algebra-division-fractions">
  <title>Algebraic Division and Fractions</title>
  <section xml:id="algebra-division-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>Algebraic fractions are quotients of expressions. Factoring can reveal common multiplicative factors that may be cancelled, provided the denominator is not zero.</p></introduction>
    <p>Cancellation applies to factors in a product, not to separate terms joined by addition or subtraction. Always record restrictions on the denominator.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="algebra-division-fractions-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-algebra-division-fractions">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Simplify (6x²)/(3x) for x≠0.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The quotient is 2x for x≠0.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-algebra-division-fractions">
      <statement><p>Simplify (x²−9)/(x−3) for x≠3.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Factorise the numerator: (x−3)(x+3)/(x−3)=x+3, for x≠3.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
